      "claim": "By November 1996 the prohibition of recruiting children under the age of fifteen into armed forces or groups had attained the status of customary international law.",
      "reasoning": "Additional Protocol I to the Geneva Conventions (1977) obliges parties to refrain from recruiting persons who have not attained fifteen years of age (art. 77(2)). The Protocol was ratified by a large majority of states and was implemented through national legislation criminalising such recruitment, demonstrating both widespread state practice and opinio juris. These elements satisfy the criteria for a rule of customary international law, making the prohibition binding on all parties to an armed conflict, irrespective of treaty ratification.",
      "citations": [
        {
          "type": "treaty",
          "instrument": "ap_i",
          "article": "77(2)",
          "quoted_text": "the Parties to the conflict shall take all feasible measures in order that children who have not attained the age of fifteen years do not take a direct part in hostilities and, in particular, they shall refrain from recruiting them into their armed forces."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "claim": "The customary prohibition on recruiting children under fifteen carries individual criminal responsibility for those who enlist or use them in hostilities.",
      "reasoning": "Customary IHL imposes individual criminal liability for serious violations of its norms. The Rome Statute (1998) codifies the crime of conscripting or enlisting children under fifteen (art. 8(2)(e)(vii)), expressly stating that the conduct constitutes a war crime. The Statute’s purpose was to crystallise an already existing customary norm, not to create a novel offense. Consequently, at the time of the alleged conduct in 1996, the act already attracted individual criminal responsibility under customary law.",
      "citations": [
        {
          "type": "treaty",
          "instrument": "rome_statute",
          "article": "8(2)(e)(vii)",
          "quoted_text": "Conscripting or enlisting children under the age of fifteen years into armed forces or groups or using them to participate actively in hostilities;"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "claim": "The obligation to criminalise the recruitment of children under fifteen was already recognized as a norm of customary law before 1996, as reflected in the later Optional Protocol to the Convention on the Rights of the Child (2000).",
      "reasoning": "Article 4 of the CRC Optional Protocol obliges states to adopt legal measures necessary to prohibit and criminalise the recruitment of persons under eighteen, and specifically bars armed groups from recruiting persons under eighteen. The Protocol was drafted on the basis of an already‑existing, widely‑accepted customary rule protecting children from military recruitment; its provisions therefore confirm the pre‑existing customary status of the prohibition and the duty to criminalise it. Although the Protocol entered into force after 1996, its drafting history demonstrates that the norm was considered customary at the time of the accused’s conduct.",
      "citations": [
        {
          "type": "treaty",
          "instrument": "crc_opac",
          "article": "4",
          "quoted_text": "Armed groups that are distinct from the armed forces of a State should not, under any circumstances, recruit or use in hostilities persons under the age of 18 years."
        },
        {
          "type": "treaty",
          "instrument": "crc_opac",
          "article": "4",
          "quoted_text": "States Parties shall take all feasible measures to prevent such recruitment and use, including the adoption of legal measures necessary to prohibit and criminalize such practices."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "claim": "The prohibition against recruiting children under fifteen is a peremptory norm (jus cogens) of international law, rendering any individual who violates it liable irrespective of any domestic legal gaps.",
      "reasoning": "The special protection afforded to children under fifteen in AP I (art. 77(2)) and the universal condemnation of child recruitment reflected in the CRC Optional Protocol (art. 4) indicate a norm of such fundamental importance that it is accepted as jus cogens. Peremptory norms admit no derogation and generate individual criminal responsibility even where domestic law is silent. Therefore, the accused’s conduct constituted a breach of a jus cogens rule, attracting individual liability under customary international law.",
      "citations": [
        {
          "type": "treaty",
          "instrument": "ap_i",
          "article": "77(2)",
          "quoted_text": "the Parties to the conflict shall take all feasible measures in order that children who have not attained the age of fifteen years do not take a direct part in hostilities and, in particular, they shall refrain from recruiting them into their armed forces."
        },
        {
          "type": "treaty",
          "instrument": "crc_opac",
          "article": "4",
          "quoted_text": "Armed groups that are distinct from the armed forces of a State should not, under any circumstances, recruit or use in hostilities persons under the age of 18 years."
        }
      ]
    }
