      "claim": "By November 1996 a customary norm prohibited the recruitment of children under the age of fifteen into armed forces or armed groups, and the use of such children in hostilities.",
      "reasoning": "Additional Protocol I to the Geneva Conventions, which had been ratified by the overwhelming majority of states by the mid‑1990s, obliges parties to refrain from recruiting persons who have not attained fifteen years of age (art. 77(2)). The near‑universal ratification and the inclusion of the rule in a treaty that reflects the humanitarian principles of the Geneva Conventions demonstrate both widespread state practice and opinio juris, satisfying the criteria for a rule of customary international law. Consequently, the prohibition was binding on all parties to an armed conflict, irrespective of treaty ratification, by the relevant date.",
      "citations": [
        {
          "type": "treaty",
          "instrument": "ap_i",
          "article": "77(2)",
          "quoted_text": "The Parties to the conflict shall take all feasible measures in order that children who have not attained the age of fifteen years do not take a direct part in hostilities and, in particular, they shall refrain from recruiting them into their armed forces."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "claim": "The existence of that customary prohibition did not, by November 1996, entail individual criminal responsibility for the act of recruiting children under fifteen; the norm that such conduct constitutes a war crime with personal liability was only crystallised later, as reflected by the 1998 Rome Statute.",
      "reasoning": "While the customary prohibition was established, the international community had not yet articulated a corresponding individual criminal liability. The first treaty to expressly label the recruitment of children under fifteen as a war crime is the Rome Statute (1998), which enumerates the conduct in art. 8(2)(e)(vii). The fact that the norm was only codified in 1998 indicates that, prior to that date, customary IHL did not yet impose a specific individual criminal responsibility for the act. Moreover, the Optional Protocol to the Convention on the Rights of the Child (2000) obliges states to adopt legal measures to criminalise such recruitment (art. 4), showing that the requirement to criminalise was a later development and not yet a universally accepted customary rule before 1996. Therefore, at the time of the accused’s conduct, the act was prohibited but did not automatically give rise to individual criminal liability under customary international law.",
      "citations": [
        {
          "type": "treaty",
          "instrument": "rome_statute",
          "article": "8(2)(e)(vii)",
          "quoted_text": "Conscripting or enlisting children under the age of fifteen years into armed forces or groups or using them to participate actively in hostilities;"
        },
        {
          "type": "treaty",
          "instrument": "crc_opac",
          "article": "4",
          "quoted_text": "States Parties shall take all feasible measures to prevent such recruitment and use, including the adoption of legal measures necessary to prohibit and criminalize such practices."
        }
      ]
    }
  ]
}

